fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1000", description: some(value: "Implement proposal 059 R3: classify the direct content of every secondary entry with default-deny semantics. A secondary entry may add members and spec claims, never identity.\n\nOPERATION RULE: an operation declared in a secondary entry must be fresh and must have a runnable Anthill body. Refuse a bodyless declaration even if a host mapping could later back it. A `[simp]` equation is not an operation body. Duplicate declarations across main and secondary entries are already refused by WI-1049 and are a control for this ticket, not another implementation path. Ordinary namespaces and bodyless operations in a main entry remain legal.\n\nALLOWED: fresh body-having operations and operation-block sugar; consts; nested sort or enum declarations with bodies, whose own addresses make them main entries; same-entry proofs; `provides` declarations and host realization blocks. The interior of a host `provides` block is recursively classified.\n\nREFUSED: entities; type-parameter declarations; sort-level requires; every rule until WI-1001 can enforce the narrow fresh-head rule; every fact and constraint except the explicit `provides` spelling; foreign-target proofs; and any unclassified production.\n\nCLASSIFY BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION: bodyless type aliases, standalone type-parameter binders, and `describe` declarations. Imports are governed separately by WI-995.\n\nACCEPTANCE: drive a fresh body-having secondary operation through dispatch and assert its value. Refuse a fresh bodyless secondary operation with a diagnostic naming the sort and declaration. Pin controls showing that a main-entry bodyless host-facing operation remains legal, a duplicate declaration is refused by WI-1049, and ordinary namespaces retain their existing content rules. Exercise the same operation rule through operation-block sugar. Run rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-10T07:08:10Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1000", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. Commit 030d3e31. Full workspace 4475 green (29 binaries); tree-sitter corpus 193/193.\n\nTHE THREE UNCLASSIFIED PRODUCTIONS — each decided on a MEASUREMENT, and one of the\nmeasurements is the whole argument:\n * BODYLESS TYPE ALIAS -> ALLOWED. `sort Code = Int64` in an entry, used as a sibling\n   operation's return type, loads and the operation ANSWERS. It declares a new type at\n   its own address, which is the nested-sort reason; \"with a body\" in 059's allowed\n   list was there to exclude the `= ?` binder beside it.\n * TYPE-PARAMETER BINDER -> REFUSED, all four spellings (they are one IR after WI-454,\n   so the check reads `is_type_param`, not the presence of a body). The harm is not\n   only \"identity\": measured, `sort T = ?` in an entry reaches `add_type_param` while\n   `SortInfo(Rec).parameters` stays nil, against `[T]` for the identical main-entry\n   declaration. WI-1008's split again, in the one field R3 says must never be written\n   from an entry — so refusing is the answer rather than completing the record.\n * `describe` -> the PROOF's condition VERBATIM (target in the same entry). Of the two\n   objections 059 raises, only the target one bites: the fact objection does not single\n   `describe` out, because the inline `{< … >}` block R3 calls \"inert and always\n   allowed\" asserts the very same DescriptionInfo predicate from a nested sort in the\n   same entry. And refusing outright would cost a capability rather than nothing —\n   MEASURED, an operation's own inline description emits ZERO DescriptionInfo (the\n   grammar parses `description` on operation_declaration; parse::ir::Operation has no\n   field), so a standalone `describe` is today the only way to document a member. That\n   drop is now WI-1070, and the verdict survives it: the rule is keyed on the TARGET,\n   not on the absence of an alternative.\n\nTHE GRAMMAR CHANGE WAS FORCED, NOT CHOSEN, and the user challenged it twice — rightly.\nR3 refuses `fact Spec[X]` in an entry and allows `provides Spec[X]`, but\n`provides_clause` was a `_sort_content` production only: the ALLOWED spelling was\nunwritable in the one place 059 calls the point of the mechanism, and the refusal alone\nwould have retracted a delivered capability (four existing tests broke on exactly that\n— wi1008 fixture 3, wi978 x3). `_namespace_content` now admits it; since the grammar\ncannot tell an entry from an ordinary namespace (an ADDRESS question, not a text one),\nthe loader classifies — `ProvidesClauseNeedsSort` where no sort occupies the address.\n\nMY PREMISE WAS WRONG AND THE USER'S QUESTION FOUND IT. I wrote \"both spellings mean one\nthing\". Measured, they take the carrier from DIFFERENT PLACES:\n    namespace Rec { provides Show[T = Other] }  ->  (Rec,   Show)   -- ENCLOSING SCOPE\n    fact Show[T = Other]        one level out   ->  (Other, Show)   -- the BINDINGS\nSo `fact Spec[Carrier]` is the ONLY orphan-instance route and must stay. They coincide\nexactly when the carrier is the entry's own sort, and that coincidence is all the\nrefusal removes — the orphan claim moves one block outward, where nothing is refused.\nDriven by `a_claim_about_another_carrier_is_written_one_level_out`. The near-miss\n`provides Show[T = Other]` filing silently under Rec is pre-existing (a sort body does\nthe same) and is now WI-1069.\n\n/code-review AT `high` FOUND FIVE, four of them mine; every one was DRIVEN before being\nfixed, and one measurement corrected the review:\n 1. a QUALIFIED self-target was refused by a `!contains('.')` test — `describe Rec.g`\n    beside `operation g` in the same entry, refused while asserting a condition that\n    HELD. Now the prefix must name the entry's own address. The review read a nested\n    sort's member as the same case; it is not — a nested `sort … end` is the MAIN ENTRY\n    OF ITS OWN TYPE, so `Code.widen` stays foreign, and that row is in the test.\n 2. a DOTTED declaration name declares at ANOTHER address (`operation Inner.helper` ->\n    `Rec.Inner.helper`, an ordinary namespace R3 must not reach), so the dotted and\n    nested-namespace spellings of ONE declaration had opposite verdicts. Skipped now.\n    EXCEPTION, measured and NOT in the review: `sort <p>.T = ?` still registers T as a\n    type param of the ENCLOSING sort (`type_params_of_sort(Rec) == [\"T\"]` while the\n    symbol sits at `Rec.Inner.T`), so a blanket dotted skip would have opened a hole.\n 3. `record_declared_names` recorded a dotted name's LAST SEGMENT, so `proof helper`\n    beside `operation Inner.helper` was ADMITTED — the mirror of (2), and the exact hole\n    the target check exists to close.\n 4. a VALUE-LESS `const` was admitted while the identically-shaped body-less operation\n    was refused. `const_map` is the const-level peer of `operation_map` (§10.2/WI-889),\n    so R4 clause 2's reason applies verbatim. 059's allowed list said only \"consts\",\n    written before that peer was weighed; proposal and spec now say so.\n 5. `SortContent ::= … | ProvidesBlock` documented a production the parser rejects.\n\nCONTROLS, MEASURED by deleting the sub-pass and re-running rather than predicted: 12 of\n17 fail, 5 pass either way — the capability row, the main-entry bodyless operation\n(driven out of the REAL stdlib, not a fixture), WI-1049's duplicate rule still firing\nwith ITS message, the allowed-production matrix, and the orphan claim. Both matrices\nreport every row, so `every_refused_production_is_refused` names all ten when backed out\nrather than the first.\n\nTHREE EXISTING FIXTURES WROTE REFUSED SHAPES — each a real update, none silenced:\nwi956_kind_gate_tests (bodyless `peek` in an entry -> `= x`; `Ord.look` beside it stays\nbodyless as the main-entry control), wi1008 fixture (3) and wi978's `fixture()`\n(`fact` -> `provides`). wi978's pair now differs in SPELLING as well as placement — the\nouter claim must stay `fact`, since `provides` there would file under the namespace —\nand that is stated at the site with why it leaves the property intact.\n\nONE PROCESS NOTE FOR WHOEVER FILES NEXT: the two follow-ups were first filed as WI-1068\n/ WI-1069 against a stale local tracker; origin had ALREADY taken WI-1068 for an\nunrelated ticket (`add` hardcodes cargo-test). Caught on `git fetch` before pushing —\n`anthill-todo add` allocates from the LOCAL file, so an id is not reserved until it is\npushed. Both were re-filed through the CLI on the rebased base and every reference moved\nwith them, in one pass so the shift could not double-apply.\n\nWHAT WI-1001 INHERITS UNCHANGED: the rule ban is still blanket, and both gates (WI-980,\nWI-895) are untouched. The entry-individuation machinery it needs is in place and\ndriven — two blocks in one file are ONE entry, the same text in two files is TWO.", at: "2026-08-10T07:08:11Z")
